TITLE: Semantics engine survey: eight candidates probed on a seventeen-row rubric

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[EVIDENCE]
UID: EV-SEMANTICS-ENGINE-ALTERNATIVES
TITLE: Semantics engine survey: eight candidates probed on a seventeen-row rubric
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
SOURCE: Claude workflow wf_29569539-fe2 on 2026-09-03 (five finder lenses, a shortlist, eight deep assessments each with a runnable probe, eight independent verifications, a synthesis); every number from gh api or nix eval; probes, verifier transcripts and synthesis.md archived untracked under private/semantics-engine/ in the worktree
STATEMENT: >>>
WHAT WAS ASSESSED. Whether a framework should replace the transitions spike
behind the three enum fields, before the real behaviour model is shaped. Five
finder lenses (Python statecharts; rules, logic and Datalog engines; graph-shape
validators and embedded graph databases; workflow and process engines; engines
outside Python, including Nix itself as the evaluator) kept 38 candidates and
rejected 35, mostly dead, archived or unpackaged. After deduplication 35
distinct projects remained; seven were shortlisted, 28 dropped with a reason
each, and a no-library baseline was added so the comparison had an honest
floor. Sismic was the runner-up statechart and was dropped at the shortlist:
it is the only library found with an invariant primitive as data and a monitor
statechart mechanism, but it is a fifth statechart entry behind a healthier
one.

HOW. Each candidate was judged on seventeen rows: three lifecycle shapes
(ordered ladder, branching, rise-only with an actor gate), four rules that
reach beyond one field (a graph invariant, a cross-edge comparison, the ripple
to a fixed point, a state tuple over two fields), commands versus events,
checkpoints with what a gate sees, flows, relation contracts, eval-time
diagnostics, rendering for the board, declarative form, Nix delivery,
in-process callability, and maintenance. Each row got one verdict: native (the
library expresses it as data or a first-class API), glue (caller code, lines
counted), or no (not expressible without reimplementing the concept). Every
candidate was probed against a toy graph shaped like this corpus: a DECISION
with and without a Superseded_By edge, two MECHANISMs at different DEPTHs, a
WORK that Crosses one, and per-edge PARENT_FP entries. Each probe carried
negative controls, so a gate that never fires could not pass as working. A
second agent that had not written the probe re-ran it and re-pulled every
maintenance number; the corrections it made are applied below.

WHAT WAS SKIPPED. Checkpoints and flows were not probed for transitions, there
being no API to point a probe at. The branching lifecycle was not probed for
pySHACL or the baseline. Nothing was measured at real corpus scale except
pySHACL, which validated the real 489-file corpus in 43 ms.

WHAT WAS MEASURED, per candidate.

transitions 0.9.3, the incumbent. Ladder, branching and mermaid native; the
actor gate, the graph gate, the cross-edge comparison and the ripple worked
only as caller code (5, 9, 16 and 26 lines, the last a floor); checkpoints,
flows and relation contracts have no API at all. Every gate body is a Python
callable or a name resolved on the model. Maintenance: 2 commits in 12 months,
none for 11.8; 1 release in 24 months; no maintainer reply on any issue or
pull request opened in 2026. The nixpkgs package propagates pygraphviz
unconditionally, a 312 MB closure at both delivery sites, for a backend the
probe showed is never imported when rendering mermaid.

python-statemachine 3.2.1. The healthiest library of the same shape: 126
commits in 12 months, seven releases, zero runtime dependencies, in nixpkgs.
Loads a machine from JSON, YAML or SCXML against a published schema, and ran
DEPTH crossed with AUTHORED_BY as one parallel-state document. Guard bodies are
method names, so every graph-reaching gate is still Python. Two silent traps:
the loader disables three of four diagnostics, and a refused send raises
nothing unless a flag is set. Checkpoints and relation contracts have no
representation.

clingo 5.8.2. Every row was expressed, but as an 85-line answer-set program
the assessor wrote; the library models no lifecycle, gate, actor or diagnostic.
Verification re-graded every native to glue. The probe's flow harness
duplicated its gate bodies, had already lost one gate in the copy, and
certified a step as taken that the command path refused. A compiled extension,
so the strictdoc venv splice would gain a Python-minor coupling.

pySHACL 0.40.1 with rdflib 7.6.0. The graph rules that broke the incumbent cost
nothing: the dangling-supersession invariant is eight lines of SHACL Core. But
there is no state, no transition and no renderer; rules only add triples (a
field held signed and drifted at once); no fixed point across shapes; the rule
layer is a non-normative 2017 note; the corpus needs a projection into RDF (30
lines, 71 ms). Bus factor one.

Nix lib.evalModules on the evaluator already in use. The whole model
serialised to a 6583-byte JSON with zero functions, because gates are data in
a closed operation enum that rejects anything else at eval time. Gate
placement was derived from what each checkpoint declares it sees and
correctly excluded a gate from a checkpoint that could not see its inputs.
Authored machinery: 231 lines of schema and 214 of engine. A full evaluation
costs 29 ms with a pinned lib and 825 ms through the flake registry.

XState 5.32.6. Fans one event to N per-edge actors in a deterministic order
that settles with no driver loop. Guards cannot be data (expression strings
throw, and composed guards vanish silently from a JSON round trip). Commands
versus events, checkpoints and relation contracts are absent. Not in nixpkgs;
a third runtime and a vendored npm lockfile; subprocess only from Python.

OPA 1.19.1. The model ran as 79 lines of JSON against one fixed 224-line Rego
interpreter carrying no rule-specific knowledge, every gate confirmed by a
mutated-graph control. It holds no state and never mutates, so the ladder, the
branch, the tuple and the write half of the ripple are outside it. A Go binary
delivered on PATH, 15 ms per call as a subprocess. The healthiest project
measured: 951 commits in 12 months, 141 contributors.

No-library baseline. A 318-line standard-library interpreter over a 250-line
JSON model covered every row, 49 assertions passing under positive and
negative controls; load and dispatch under a millisecond in-process. Seven
predicate operations exist and three appear in any gate. Two defects surfaced
during its own probe, an unreachable state and a ripple that ignored one event
kind, both caught only by the controls.

Row totals after correction, over the thirteen behaviour rows (native, glue,
no): transitions 3, 7, 3; python-statemachine 4, 7, 2; clingo 0, 13, 0;
pySHACL 4, 9, 0; Nix evalModules 9, 4, 0; XState 2, 7, 4; OPA 0, 9, 4;
baseline 7, 6, 0.

THE ARCHITECTURES THE EVIDENCE SUPPORTS. Four, assembled from measured parts:
keep the incumbent and add a graph-mechanics layer beside it; swap it for
python-statemachine; emit the model from Nix and read it with a small fixed
interpreter; or split into a lifecycle engine plus a graph-query engine. One
structural finding stands out. In every statechart library the gate body is
code, so the rows that broke the spike are caller code regardless of the
library, and the library holds only the ladder, the branch and the diagram.
And the Nix option-tree probe and the no-library baseline, run separately
against different framings, arrived at the same shape: a JSON model with a
closed predicate vocabulary plus a fixed interpreter of a few hundred lines.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT SETTLE. How many distinct gate shapes the real model needs
is unknown, and every declarative claim here holds only if that number is
small. Nothing was measured at corpus scale except pySHACL. No candidate
supplies a transaction primitive. Whether a rule library in a second language
satisfies DEC-LAYER-STACK is a reading of that decision, not a measurement.
